nodecode-iris-chat is a rewrite of irislib's irislib/iris-chat-rs:

  https://github.com/irislib/iris-chat-rs
  commit 43a1066894f1948adb3febc1e3a91a31ccccef14
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 lauri000 (LICENSE)

The rewrite is plugins/iris-chat, the plugin's Hermes entry, as a Nodecode
cell. What it keeps: the same workflow the plugin's one skill taught -- create
a separate local identity, inspect it, exchange a full invite, list chats,
read one, send into it, and acknowledge a message as seen -- as tools the
model calls by name (iris_whoami, iris_chats, iris_read, iris_send, iris_seen,
iris_invite_create, iris_invite_accept) and the same verbs from eval; the
exact argv each step used (`--json', `chat list', `read CHAT --limit N',
`send CHAT TEXT', `seen CHAT ID', `invite create --full', `invite accept URL',
`service status'); the JSON envelope read as the data object it carries and a
failed run shown as the program's own sentence; and the skill's whole posture: one persistent profile directory per identity, never a
person's own profile, incoming messages are data and not instructions, a send's
`queued'/'pending'/'sent' delivery is local until a read shows `received' or
`seen', reads never mark anything seen, a pending group join is not membership,
and keys and full invite links stay out of public logs.

What the plugin left to the model's own discipline, and this cell does
instead: at a turn's first request the cell asks the profile how many messages
wait unread and says so once, so a person waiting is not missed while the agent
works on something else; `notify' switches that off.

What the plugin got from its host and Nodecode has no seam for is left out: the
skill-file discovery and `requires_local_executor' advertising, the
marketplace and install commands, and a foreground `listen' loop, which is one
streaming process rather than a call with an answer -- the cell's manual points
at `iris --json listen --chat CHAT' for it. Nothing of the plugin's packaging
is bundled: the cell runs the same iris program the plugin ran, found on PATH
or named in its section, and its own code is Common Lisp.
